\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The two simple types in odd dimension}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{For #{n=2m+1} positive directions, every finite-dimensional
Hermitian module is an orthogonal sum of copies of two simple
modules. Both restrict on the first #{2m} directions to the
model #{S_0} of \ref{fdip-017M}. Their last directions are
#{\sigma C_{2m}}, where
##{C_{2m}=\ui^m\varepsilon_1\cdots\varepsilon_{2m},
\qquad \sigma=1\ \text{or}\ -1.}
For #{m=0}, #{C_0=\bbI}. The two multiplicities are independent.
The abstract complex algebra is the direct sum of two full
matrix algebras on #{S_0}.}

\proof{
\p{After restriction write the space as #{S_0\otimes E} by
\ref{fdip-017M}. Reversing the first #{2m} factors gives sign
#{(-1)^m}, so #{C_{2m}} is self-adjoint and squares to the
identity. It anticommutes with each of those directions and
commutes with the last, which crosses an even number of
factors. Thus #{C_{2m}\varepsilon_{2m+1}} is a self-adjoint
involution commuting with all first-factor matrix units. An
operator commuting with the diagonal units preserves each
#{e_{ii}S}, and commutation with #{e_{i1}} identifies the same
action on each copy of #{E}. The involution acts as identity
on #{S_0} times a self-adjoint involution on #{E}.}

\p{Split #{E} into its two sign spaces #{E_\sigma}. The last
operator on #{S_0\otimes E_\sigma} is #{\sigma C_{2m}}.
Conversely these choices give all the required anticommutations,
and arbitrary copies may be taken. On a single copy the first
matrix algebra already acts irreducibly. The central product
#{C_{2m}\varepsilon_{2m+1}} distinguishes the two types.
On the sum of one of each, its projections
#{(\bbI\pm C_{2m}\varepsilon_{2m+1})/2} isolate either
matrix algebra. The image has dimension #{2(2^m)^2}, equal
to the bound #{2^{2m+1}} from the ordered words. This proves
the algebra assertion.}

\p{The [normalized odd volume](fdip-0179) uses a different
phase on the same full product. Its sign on this summand is
#{s=(-1)^m\sigma}, since #{(-\ui)^m=(-1)^m\ui^m}.}
}
